(434,77,1,'assistant','**Vehicle and problem:** Honda Click 125i\n\n**Symptom:** My headlight is not working\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Offline knowledge — this answer comes from the local reviewed repair library. Gemini was not used.\n\n**What I still need to know:**\n\n- What model year is it? If you are not sure, say “not sure” and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\n\n  - Not sure\n\n**What this could indicate:**\n\n- Discharged or dead 12V motorcycle battery — Confidence: high\n\n  - Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\n\n  - If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\n\n- Blown main fuse or disconnected engine ground — Confidence: medium\n\n  - Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\n\n  - Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\n\n**Safe checks, in order:**\n\n- Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\n\n  - Expected: Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.\n\n  - If abnormal: If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\n\n  - Safety first: Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.\n\n- Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\n\n  - Expected: Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.\n\n  - If abnormal: Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\n\n  - Safety first: Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.\n\n**Tools and parts:**\n\n- Digital Multimeter (DC Voltage range)\n\n- 8mm/10mm box wrench or JIS screwdriver\n\n- wire terminal brush.\n\n- Fuse puller or needle-nose pliers\n\n- replacement 15A/20A mini blade fuse\n\n- digital multimeter.\n\n**Parts:**\n\n- 12V Motorcycle Maintenance-Free Battery (YTX5L-BS / YTZ6V) — compatibility NOT verified\n\n- Mini Blade Automotive Fuse 15A / 20A — compatibility NOT verified\n\n**Repair guidance from the record:**\n\n_Complete the checks before replacing parts. The steps below are from the stored record and may not match your exact variant._\n\n- [ ] 1. Switch motorcycle ignition OFF and set kill switch to OFF.\n\n- [ ] 2. Access battery compartment (under seat or floorboard panel).\n\n- [ ] 3. Inspect battery terminals for white or greenish sulfate corrosion; disconnect NEGATIVE (-) lead first, then POSITIVE (+).\n\n- [ ] 4. Clean cable lugs and lead posts with wire brush and baking soda solution.\n\n- [ ] 5. Reconnect POSITIVE (+) first, then NEGATIVE (-); tighten firmly and torque terminal bolts to 4–5 Nm.\n\n- [ ] 6. Charge battery with a smart trickle charger at 0.8A–1.2A until fully topped at 12.8V DC.\n\n- [ ] 7. Re-test starter push with headlight ON; verify engine cranks briskly.\n\n- [ ] 8. Switch ignition OFF and locate fuse box near battery.\n\n- [ ] 9. Pull the 15A/20A MAIN fuse using plastic fuse puller.\n\n- [ ] 10. Hold fuse against light; inspect internal bridge wire.\n\n- [ ] 11. If broken, inspect wiring harness for pinched or rubbed bare wires grounding against frame.\n\n- [ ] 12. Insert new fuse of the EXACT same ampere rating (never bypass with foil or wire).\n\n- [ ] 13. Turn ignition ON; verify gauge cluster sweeps and headlight illuminates.\n\n**How to verify:**\n\n- Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.\n\n- Stop and seek professional help: If battery will not hold 12.5V after a full 4-hour charge or drops instantly, the internal lead-acid plates have sulfated and battery must be replaced.\n\n- Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.\n\n- Stop and seek professional help: If replacement fuse blows instantly when ignition turns on, a dead short circuit exists; stop and consult a qualified technician.\n\n**Sources in the local library:**\n\n- Battery Council International (BCI) Technical Guidelines & Motorcycle Service Manual Section 16 (Electrical) — reviewed\n\n- OEM Electrical Troubleshooting Guide & SAE J1284 Blade Type Fuses standard — reviewed\n\n_Technical procedure text is kept close to its source wording to avoid changing measurements or safety meaning._\n\n**Limitations:**\n\n- This is offline guidance from stored records, not a confirmed diagnosis.\n\n- The stored record may not cover your exact model, year, or variant. Verify part numbers and specifications against the manufacturer information for your vehicle.\n\n**Next action:** What model year is it? If you are not sure, say “not sure” and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\n\n[chips: Not sure]','english','{\"reply_text\": \"**Vehicle and problem:** Honda Click 125i\\n\\n**Symptom:** My headlight is not working\\n\\n> [!NOTE]\\n> Offline knowledge \\u2014 this answer comes from the local reviewed repair library. Gemini was not used.\\n\\n**What I still need to know:**\\n\\n- What model year is it? If you are not sure, say \\u201cnot sure\\u201d and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\\n\\n  - Not sure\\n\\n**What this could indicate:**\\n\\n- Discharged or dead 12V motorcycle battery \\u2014 Confidence: high\\n\\n  - Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\\n\\n  - If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\\n\\n- Blown main fuse or disconnected engine ground \\u2014 Confidence: medium\\n\\n  - Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\\n\\n  - Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\\n\\n**Safe checks, in order:**\\n\\n- Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\\n\\n  - Expected: Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.\\n\\n  - If abnormal: If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\\n\\n  - Safety first: Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.\\n\\n- Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\\n\\n  - Expected: Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.\\n\\n  - If abnormal: Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\\n\\n  - Safety first: Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.\\n\\n**Tools and parts:**\\n\\n- Digital Multimeter (DC Voltage range)\\n\\n- 8mm/10mm box wrench or JIS screwdriver\\n\\n- wire terminal brush.\\n\\n- Fuse puller or needle-nose pliers\\n\\n- replacement 15A/20A mini blade fuse\\n\\n- digital multimeter.\\n\\n**Parts:**\\n\\n- 12V Motorcycle Maintenance-Free Battery (YTX5L-BS / YTZ6V) \\u2014 compatibility NOT verified\\n\\n- Mini Blade Automotive Fuse 15A / 20A \\u2014 compatibility NOT verified\\n\\n**Repair guidance from the record:**\\n\\n_Complete the checks before replacing parts. The steps below are from the stored record and may not match your exact variant._\\n\\n- [ ] 1. Switch motorcycle ignition OFF and set kill switch to OFF.\\n\\n- [ ] 2. Access battery compartment (under seat or floorboard panel).\\n\\n- [ ] 3. Inspect battery terminals for white or greenish sulfate corrosion; disconnect NEGATIVE (-) lead first, then POSITIVE (+).\\n\\n- [ ] 4. Clean cable lugs and lead posts with wire brush and baking soda solution.\\n\\n- [ ] 5. Reconnect POSITIVE (+) first, then NEGATIVE (-); tighten firmly and torque terminal bolts to 4\\u20135 Nm.\\n\\n- [ ] 6. Charge battery with a smart trickle charger at 0.8A\\u20131.2A until fully topped at 12.8V DC.\\n\\n- [ ] 7. Re-test starter push with headlight ON; verify engine cranks briskly.\\n\\n- [ ] 8. Switch ignition OFF and locate fuse box near battery.\\n\\n- [ ] 9. Pull the 15A/20A MAIN fuse using plastic fuse puller.\\n\\n- [ ] 10. Hold fuse against light; inspect internal bridge wire.\\n\\n- [ ] 11. If broken, inspect wiring harness for pinched or rubbed bare wires grounding against frame.\\n\\n- [ ] 12. Insert new fuse of the EXACT same ampere rating (never bypass with foil or wire).\\n\\n- [ ] 13. Turn ignition ON; verify gauge cluster sweeps and headlight illuminates.\\n\\n**How to verify:**\\n\\n- Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.\\n\\n- Stop and seek professional help: If battery will not hold 12.5V after a full 4-hour charge or drops instantly, the internal lead-acid plates have sulfated and battery must be replaced.\\n\\n- Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.\\n\\n- Stop and seek professional help: If replacement fuse blows instantly when ignition turns on, a dead short circuit exists; stop and consult a qualified technician.\\n\\n**Sources in the local library:**\\n\\n- Battery Council International (BCI) Technical Guidelines & Motorcycle Service Manual Section 16 (Electrical) \\u2014 reviewed\\n\\n- OEM Electrical Troubleshooting Guide & SAE J1284 Blade Type Fuses standard \\u2014 reviewed\\n\\n_Technical procedure text is kept close to its source wording to avoid changing measurements or safety meaning._\\n\\n**Limitations:**\\n\\n- This is offline guidance from stored records, not a confirmed diagnosis.\\n\\n- The stored record may not cover your exact model, year, or variant. Verify part numbers and specifications against the manufacturer information for your vehicle.\\n\\n**Next action:** What model year is it? If you are not sure, say \\u201cnot sure\\u201d and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\\n\\n[chips: Not sure]\", \"language\": \"english\", \"intent\": \"diagnostic_offline\", \"diagnostic_stage\": \"tests\", \"vehicle_profile\": {\"vehicle_type\": \"motorcycle\", \"make\": \"Honda\", \"model\": \"Click 125i\", \"year\": \"\", \"variant\": \"\", \"engine_displacement\": \"\", \"fuel_system\": \"\"}, \"symptoms\": [\"My headlight is not working\"], \"missing_questions\": [{\"question\": \"What model year is it? If you are not sure, say \\u201cnot sure\\u201d and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\", \"why\": \"Model year changes parts and procedures on many vehicles.\", \"options\": [\"Not sure\"]}], \"safety\": {\"severity\": \"none\", \"summary\": \"\", \"warnings\": [], \"stop_use\": false}, \"possible_causes\": [{\"cause\": \"Discharged or dead 12V motorcycle battery\", \"confidence\": \"high\", \"evidence\": \"Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\", \"uncertainty\": \"If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\"}, {\"cause\": \"Blown main fuse or disconnected engine ground\", \"confidence\": \"medium\", \"evidence\": \"Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\", \"uncertainty\": \"Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\"}], \"findings\": \"\", \"visible_evidence\": [], \"diagnostic_tests\"