(456,80,1,'assistant','**Vehicle and problem:** Honda Click\n\n**Symptom:** can i see a picture of honda click\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Offline knowledge — this answer comes from the local reviewed repair library. Gemini was not used.\n\n**What I still need to know:**\n\n- What model year is it? If you are not sure, say “not sure” and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\n\n  - Not sure\n\n**What this could indicate:**\n\n- Air trapped inside hydraulic brake lines or degraded brake fluid — Confidence: high\n\n  - Squeeze/press brake lever 5 times rapidly. If lever firms up temporarily but goes soft after resting 10 seconds, air is trapped.\n\n  - If lever continues to sink slowly while held under steady pressure, internal master cylinder piston cup seals are bypassing.\n\n- Worn brake pad friction material past service limit (<2.0mm) — Confidence: high\n\n  - Inspect brake caliper window using flashlight to measure remaining pad friction material thickness against steel backing plate.\n\n  - Friction material under 1.5mm–2.0mm or wear groove gone requires immediate pad replacement before steel plate scores rotor.\n\n**Safe checks, in order:**\n\n- Squeeze/press brake lever 5 times rapidly. If lever firms up temporarily but goes soft after resting 10 seconds, air is trapped.\n\n  - Expected: Brake lever should become firm within 15–20mm of travel and hold solid pressure for 60 seconds without sinking.\n\n  - If abnormal: If lever continues to sink slowly while held under steady pressure, internal master cylinder piston cup seals are bypassing.\n\n  - Safety first: CRITICAL SAFETY HAZARD: DO NOT RIDE OR DRIVE. Operating a vehicle with soft or spongy brakes can cause catastrophic collision.\n\n- Inspect brake caliper window using flashlight to measure remaining pad friction material thickness against steel backing plate.\n\n  - Expected: Pad friction material thickness must be 3.0mm to 5.0mm. Wear indicator groove should be clearly visible.\n\n  - If abnormal: Friction material under 1.5mm–2.0mm or wear groove gone requires immediate pad replacement before steel plate scores rotor.\n\n  - Safety first: CRITICAL SAFETY HAZARD: DO NOT RIDE OR DRIVE. Operating a vehicle with soft or spongy brakes can cause catastrophic collision.\n\n**Tools and parts:**\n\n- 8mm or 10mm flare nut/box wrench\n\n- 1/4-inch clear vinyl tubing\n\n- catch bottle\n\n- fresh DOT 4 brake fluid (unopened)\n\n- lint-free shop towels.\n\n- Hex/Allen key (5mm/6mm)\n\n- 12mm/14mm caliper mount socket\n\n- brake cleaner spray\n\n- C-clamp or piston spreader\n\n- 240-grit sandpaper.\n\n**Parts:**\n\n- DOT 4 High-Performance Brake Fluid (500ml) — compatibility NOT verified\n\n- Transparent Vinyl Bleeder Tubing 1/4-inch (1 meter) — compatibility NOT verified\n\n- Front Sintered Disc Brake Pads Set — compatibility NOT verified\n\n- Brake System Aerosol Cleaner (450ml) — compatibility NOT verified\n\n**Repair guidance from the record:**\n\n_Complete the checks before replacing parts. The steps below are from the stored record and may not match your exact variant._\n\n- [ ] 1. Park vehicle on flat level ground; turn handlebars to make master cylinder reservoir level.\n\n- [ ] 2. Wipe reservoir cap clean of dust; remove screws, plastic plate, and rubber diaphragm.\n\n- [ ] 3. Fill reservoir to UPPER line with fresh DOT 4 fluid from an unopened container.\n\n- [ ] 4. Slip 8mm box wrench over caliper bleeder nipple, then firmly attach clear vinyl hose leading into catch bottle.\n\n- [ ] 5. Pump brake lever 3 to 4 times smoothly, then HOLD lever squeezed firmly against grip.\n\n- [ ] 6. Crack bleeder nipple open 1/4 turn for 1 second; watch fluid and air bubbles expel into hose.\n\n- [ ] 7. Tighten bleeder nipple securely BEFORE releasing brake lever grip.\n\n- [ ] 8. Repeat cycle: pump, hold, crack open, close nipple, release lever. Never let reservoir run empty.\n\n- [ ] 9. Continue until only solid, bubble-free amber fluid emerges.\n\n- [ ] 10. Top up to UPPER line, reinstall rubber diaphragm and cover. Torque bleeder nipple to 6–8 Nm.\n\n- [ ] 11. Support vehicle safely; loosen caliper mounting bolts with socket wrench.\n\n- [ ] 12. Slide caliper assembly carefully off the brake rotor disc.\n\n- [ ] 13. Remove pad retaining pins or clips and extract worn brake pads.\n\n- [ ] 14. Clean caliper piston and slide pins with dedicated residue-free brake cleaner spray.\n\n- [ ] 15. Push caliper pistons gently and squarely back into bore to clear space for thicker new pads.\n\n- [ ] 16. Install fresh sintered or ceramic-metallic brake pads; secure retaining pin and cotter clip.\n\n- [ ] 17. Remount caliper to fork/knuckle; torque mounting bolts to OEM specification (typically 25–30 Nm for bikes, 70–90 Nm for cars).\n\n- [ ] 18. PUMP BRAKE LEVER/PEDAL 5 TIMES BEFORE MOVING to reseat pads against rotor.\n\n**How to verify:**\n\n- Brake lever should become firm within 15–20mm of travel and hold solid pressure for 60 seconds without sinking.\n\n- Stop and seek professional help: If bleeding fails to firm up lever after 10 cycles, master cylinder piston seals or caliper internal seals are damaged and require rebuilding by a certified workshop.\n\n- Pad friction material thickness must be 3.0mm to 5.0mm. Wear indicator groove should be clearly visible.\n\n- Stop and seek professional help: If rotor disc has deep grooving (>0.5mm lip) or is discolored blue from severe heat, rotor must be measured with micrometer and resurfaced or replaced.\n\n**Sources in the local library:**\n\n- FMVSS 116 / SAE J1704 Brake Fluids & Factory Workshop Hydraulic Service Manual — reviewed\n\n- ISO 611 Road Vehicles Braking Systems & OEM Workshop Brake Service Chapter — reviewed\n\n_Technical procedure text is kept close to its source wording to avoid changing measurements or safety meaning._\n\n**Limitations:**\n\n- This is offline guidance from stored records, not a confirmed diagnosis.\n\n- The stored record may not cover your exact model, year, or variant. Verify part numbers and specifications against the manufacturer information for your vehicle.\n\n**Next action:** What model year is it? If you are not sure, say “not sure” and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\n\n[chips: Not sure]','english','{\"reply_text\": \"**Vehicle and problem:** Honda Click\\n\\n**Symptom:** can i see a picture of honda click\\n\\n> [!NOTE]\\n> Offline knowledge \\u2014 this answer comes from the local reviewed repair library. Gemini was not used.\\n\\n**What I still need to know:**\\n\\n- What model year is it? If you are not sure, say \\u201cnot sure\\u201d and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\\n\\n  - Not sure\\n\\n**What this could indicate:**\\n\\n- Air trapped inside hydraulic brake lines or degraded brake fluid \\u2014 Confidence: high\\n\\n  - Squeeze/press brake lever 5 times rapidly. If lever firms up temporarily but goes soft after resting 10 seconds, air is trapped.\\n\\n  - If lever continues to sink slowly while held under steady pressure, internal master cylinder piston cup seals are bypassing.\\n\\n- Worn brake pad friction material past service limit (<2.0mm) \\u2014 Confidence: high\\n\\n  - Inspect brake caliper window using flashlight to measure remaining pad friction material thickness against steel backing plate.\\n\\n  - Friction material under 1.5mm\\u20132.0mm or wear groove gone requires immediate pad replacement before steel plate scores rotor.\\n\\n**Safe checks, in order:**\\n\\n- Squeeze/press brake lever 5 times rapidly. If lever firms up temporarily but goes soft after resting 10 seconds, air is trapped.\\n\\n  - Expected: Brake lever should become firm within 15\\u201320mm of travel and hold solid pressure for 60 seconds without sinking.\\n\\n  - If abnormal: If lever continues to sink slowly while held under steady pressure, internal master cylinder piston cup seals are bypassing.\\n\\n  - Safety first: CRITICAL SAFETY HAZARD: DO NOT RIDE OR DRIVE. Operating a vehicle with soft or spongy brakes can cause catastrophic collision.\\n\\n- Inspect brake caliper window using flashlight to measure remaining pad friction material thickness against steel backing plate.\\n\\n  - Expected: Pad friction material thickness must be 3.0mm to 5.0mm. Wear indicator groove should be clearly visible.\\n\\n  - If abnormal: Friction material under 1.5mm\\u20132.0mm or wear groove gone requires immediate pad replacement before steel plate scores rotor.\\n\\n  - Safety first: CRITICAL SAFETY HAZARD: DO NOT RIDE OR DRIVE. Operating a vehicle with soft or spongy brakes can cause catastrophic collision.\\n\\n**Tools and parts:**\\n\\n- 8mm or 10mm flare nut/box wrench\\n\\n- 1/4-inch clear vinyl tubing\\n\\n- catch bottle\\n\\n- fresh DOT 4 brake fluid (unopened)\\n\\n- lint-free shop towels.\\n\\n- Hex/Allen key (5mm/6mm)\\n\\n- 12mm/14mm caliper mount socket\\n\\n- brake cleaner spray\\n\\n- C-clamp or piston spreader\\n\\n- 240-grit sandpaper.\\n\\n**Parts:**\\n\\n- DOT 4 High-Performance Brake Fluid (500ml) \\u2014 compatibility NOT verified\\n\\n- Transparent Vinyl Bleeder Tubing 1/4-inch (1 meter) \\u2014 compatibility NOT verified\\n\\n- Front Sintered Disc Brake Pads Set \\u2014 compatibility NOT verified\\n\\n- Brake System Aerosol Cleaner (450ml) \\u2014 compatibility NOT verified\\n\\n**Repair guidance from the record:**\\n\\n_Complete the checks before replacing parts. The steps below are from the stored record and may not match your exact variant._\\n\\n- [ ] 1. Park vehicle on flat level ground; turn handlebars to make master cylinder reservoir level.\\n\\n- [ ] 2. Wipe reservoir cap clean of dust; remove screws, plastic plate, and rubber diaphragm.\\n\\n- [ ] 3. Fill reservoir to UPPER line with fresh DOT 4 fluid from an unopened container.\\n\\n- [ ] 4. Slip 8mm box wrench over caliper bleeder nipple, then firmly attach clear vinyl hose leading into catch bottle.\\n\\n- [ ] 5. Pump brake lever 3 to 4 times smoothly, then HOLD lever squeezed firmly against grip.\\n\\n- [ ] 6. Crack bleeder nipple open 1/4 turn for 1 second; watch fluid and air bubbles expel into hose.\\n\\n- [ ] 7. Tighten bleeder nipple securely BEFORE releasing brake lever grip.\\n\\n- [ ] 8. Repeat cycle: pump, hold, crack open, close nipple, release lever. Never let reservoir run empty.\\n\\n- [ ] 9. Continue until only solid, bubble-free amber fluid emerges.\\n\\n- [ ] 10. Top up to UPPER line, reinstall rubber diaphragm and cover. Torque bleeder nipple to 6\\u20138 Nm.\\n\\n- [ ] 11. Support vehicle safely; loosen caliper mounting bolts with socket wrench.\\n\\n- [ ] 12. Slide caliper assembly carefully off the brake rotor disc.\\n\\n- [ ] 13. Remove pad retaining pins or clips and extract worn brake pads.\\n\\n- [ ] 14. Clean caliper piston and slide pins with dedicated residue-free brake cleaner spray.\\n\\n- [ ] 15. Push caliper pistons gently and squarely back into bore to clear space for thicker new pads.\\n\\n- [ ] 16. Install fresh sintered or ceramic-metallic brake pads; secure retaining pin and cotter clip.\\n\\n- [ ] 17. Remount caliper to fork/knuckle; torque mounting bolts to OEM specification (typically 25\\u201330 Nm for bikes, 70\\u201390 Nm for cars).\\n\\n- [ ] 18. PUMP BRAKE LEVER/PEDAL 5 TIMES BEFORE MOVING to reseat pads against rotor.\\n\\n**How to verify:**\\n\\n- Brake lever should become firm within 15\\u201320mm of travel and hold solid pressure for 60 seconds without sinking.\\n\\n- Stop and seek professional help: If bleeding fails to firm up lever after 10 cycles, master cylinder piston seals or caliper internal seals are damaged and require rebuilding by a certified workshop.\\n\\n- Pad friction material thickness must be 3.0mm to 5.0mm. Wear indicator groove should be clearly visible.\\n\\n- Stop and seek professional help: If rotor disc has deep grooving (>0.5mm lip) or is discolored blue from severe heat, rotor must be measured with micrometer and resurfaced or replaced.\\n\\n**Sources in the local library:**\\n\\n- FMVSS 116 / SAE J1704 Brake Fluids & Factory Workshop Hydraulic Service Manual \\u2014 reviewed\\n\\n- ISO 611 Road Vehicles Braking Systems & OEM Workshop Brake Service Chapter \\u2014 reviewed\\n\\n_Technical procedure text is kept close to its source wording to avoid changing measurements or safety meaning._\\n\\n**Limitations:**\\n\\n- This is offline guidance from stored records, not a confirmed diagnosis.\\n\\n- The stored record may not cover your exact model, year, or variant. Verify part numbers and specifications against the manufacturer information for your vehicle.\\n\\n**Next action:** What model year is it? If you are not sure, say \\u201cnot sure\\u201d and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\\n\\n[chips: Not sure]\", \"language\": \"english\", \"intent\": \"diagnostic_offline\", \"diagnostic_stage\": \"tests\", \"vehicle_profile\": {\"vehicle_type\": \"motorcycle\", \"make\": \"Honda\", \"model\": \"Click\", \"year\": \"\", \"variant\": \"\", \"engine_displacement\": \"125cc\", \"fuel_system\": \"fi\"}, \"symptoms\": [\"can i see a picture of honda click\"], \"missing_questions\": [{\"question\": \"What model year is it? If you are not sure, say \\u201cnot sure\\u201d and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\", \"why\": \"Model year changes parts and procedures on many vehicles.\", \"options\": [\"Not sure\"]}], \"safety\": {\"severity\": \"none\", \"summary\": \"\", \"warnings\": [], \"stop_use\": false}, \"possible_causes\": [{\"cause\": \"Air trapped inside hydraulic brake lines or degraded brake fluid\", \"confidence\": \"high\", \"evidence\": \"Squeeze/press brake lever 5 times rapidly. If lever firms up temporarily but goes soft after resting 10 seconds, air is trapped.\", \"uncertainty\": \"If lever continues to sink slowly while held under steady pressure, internal master cylinder piston cup seals are bypassing.\"}, {\"cause\": \"Worn brake pad friction material past service limit (<2.0mm)\", \"confidence\": \"high\", \"evidence\": \"Inspect brake caliper window using flashlight to measure remaining pad friction material thickness against steel backing plate.\", \"uncertainty\": \"Friction material under 1.5mm\\u20132.0mm or wear groove gone requires immediate pad replacement before steel plate scores rotor.\"}], \"findings\": \"\", \"visible_evidence\": [], \"diagnostic_tests\": [{\"name\": \"Squeeze/press brake lever 5 times rapidly. If lever firms up temporarily but goes soft after resting 10 seconds, air is trapped.\", \"purpose\": \"Air trapped inside hydraulic brake lines or degraded brake fluid\", \"tools\": [\"8mm or 10mm flare nut/box wrench\", \"1/4-inch clear vinyl tubing\", \"catch bottle\", \"fresh DOT 4 brake fluid (unopened)\", \"lint-free shop towels.\"], \"expected\": \"Brake lever should become firm within 15\\u201320mm of travel and hold solid pressure for 60 seconds without sinking.\", \"if_abnormal\": \"If lever continues to sink slowly while held under steady pressure, internal master cylinder piston cup seals are bypassing.\", \"safety\": \"CRITICAL SAFETY HAZARD: DO NOT RIDE OR DRIVE. Operating a vehicle with soft or spongy brakes can cause catastrophic collision.\"}, {\"name\": \"Inspect brake caliper window using flashlight to measure remaining pad friction material thickness against steel backing plate.\", \"purpose\": \"Worn brake pad friction material past service limit (<2.0mm)\", \"tools\": [\"Hex/Allen key (5mm/6mm)\", \"12mm/14mm caliper mount socket\", \"brake cleaner spray\", \"C-clamp or piston spreader\", \"240-grit sandpaper.\"], \"expected\": \"Pad friction material thickness must be 3.0mm to 5.0mm. Wear indicator groove should be clearly visible.\", \"if_abnormal\": \"Friction material under 1.5mm\\u20132.0mm or wear groove gone requires immediate pad replacement before steel plate scores rotor.\", \"safety\": \"CRITICAL SAFETY HAZARD: DO NOT RIDE OR DRIVE. Operating a vehicle with soft or spongy brakes can cause catastrophic collision.\"}], \"required_tools\