\": \"Tighten bleeder nipple securely BEFORE releasing brake lever grip.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 8, \"instruction\": \"Repeat cycle: pump, hold, crack open, close nipple, release lever. Never let reservoir run empty.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 9, \"instruction\": \"Continue until only solid, bubble-free amber fluid emerges.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 10, \"instruction\": \"Top up to UPPER line, reinstall rubber diaphragm and cover. Torque bleeder nipple to 6\\u20138 Nm.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 11, \"instruction\": \"Support vehicle safely; loosen caliper mounting bolts with socket wrench.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 12, \"instruction\": \"Slide caliper assembly carefully off the brake rotor disc.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 13, \"instruction\": \"Remove pad retaining pins or clips and extract worn brake pads.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 14, \"instruction\": \"Clean caliper piston and slide pins with dedicated residue-free brake cleaner spray.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 15, \"instruction\": \"Push caliper pistons gently and squarely back into bore to clear space for thicker new pads.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 16, \"instruction\": \"Install fresh sintered or ceramic-metallic brake pads; secure retaining pin and cotter clip.\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 17, \"instruction\": \"Remount caliper to fork/knuckle; torque mounting bolts to OEM specification (typically 25\\u201330 Nm for bikes, 70\\u201390 Nm for cars).\", \"safety\": \"\"}, {\"step\": 18, \"instruction\": \"PUMP BRAKE LEVER/PEDAL 5 TIMES BEFORE MOVING to reseat pads against rotor.\", \"safety\": \"\"}], \"verification\": [\"Brake lever should become firm within 15\\u201320mm of travel and hold solid pressure for 60 seconds without sinking.\", \"Stop and seek professional help: If bleeding fails to firm up lever after 10 cycles, master cylinder piston seals or caliper internal seals are damaged and require rebuilding by a certified workshop.\", \"Pad friction material thickness must be 3.0mm to 5.0mm. Wear indicator groove should be clearly visible.\", \"Stop and seek professional help: If rotor disc has deep grooving (>0.5mm lip) or is discolored blue from severe heat, rotor must be measured with micrometer and resurfaced or replaced.\"], \"sources\": [{\"title\": \"FMVSS 116 / SAE J1704 Brake Fluids & Factory Workshop Hydraulic Service Manual\", \"reference\": \"FMVSS 116 / SAE J1704 Brake Fluids & Factory Workshop Hydraulic Service Manual\", \"reviewed\": true, \"date\": \"\"}, {\"title\": \"ISO 611 Road Vehicles Braking Systems & OEM Workshop Brake Service Chapter\", \"reference\": \"ISO 611 Road Vehicles Braking Systems & OEM Workshop Brake Service Chapter\", \"reviewed\": true, \"date\": \"\"}], \"next_action\": \"What model year is it? If you are not sure, say \\u201cnot sure\\u201d and I will avoid year-specific specifications.\", \"related_components\": [\"brakes\"], \"limitations\": [\"This is offline guidance from stored records, not a confirmed diagnosis.\", \"The stored record may not cover your exact model, year, or variant. Verify part numbers and specifications against the manufacturer information for your vehicle.\", \"This is guidance from an automated assistant, not a confirmed diagnosis or a substitute for an in-person inspection.\"], \"offline\": true, \"offline_reason\": \"Gemini is not configured on this server.\", \"chips\": [\"Not sure\"]}','offline','none','2026-11-10 00:49:17.344192'),
(457,81,1,'user','My motorcycle engine cranks normally but won\'t start or fire up.','english',NULL,'offline','none','2026-11-10 00:50:30.054741'),
(458,81,1,'assistant','**Symptom:** My motorcycle engine cranks normally but won\'t start or fire up.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Offline knowledge — this answer comes from the local reviewed repair library. Gemini was not used.\n\n**What I still need to know:**\n\n- What is your motorcycle make, model, and year? (e.g. Click 125i, NMAX 155, Barako 175).\n\n  - Honda Click 125i\n\n  - Yamaha NMAX 155\n\n  - Not sure\n\n**What this could indicate:**\n\n- Discharged or dead 12V motorcycle battery — Confidence: high\n\n  - Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\n\n  - If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\n\n- Blown main fuse or disconnected engine ground — Confidence: medium\n\n  - Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\n\n  - Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\n\n**Safe checks, in order:**\n\n- Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\n\n  - Expected: Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.\n\n  - If abnormal: If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\n\n  - Safety first: Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.\n\n- Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\n\n  - Expected: Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.\n\n  - If abnormal: Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\n\n  - Safety first: Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.\n\n**Tools and parts:**\n\n- Digital Multimeter (DC Voltage range)\n\n- 8mm/10mm box wrench or JIS screwdriver\n\n- wire terminal brush.\n\n- Fuse puller or needle-nose pliers\n\n- replacement 15A/20A mini blade fuse\n\n- digital multimeter.\n\n**Parts:**\n\n- 12V Motorcycle Maintenance-Free Battery (YTX5L-BS / YTZ6V) — compatibility NOT verified\n\n- Mini Blade Automotive Fuse 15A / 20A — compatibility NOT verified\n\n**Repair guidance from the record:**\n\n_Complete the checks before replacing parts. The steps below are from the stored record and may not match your exact variant._\n\n- [ ] 1. Switch motorcycle ignition OFF and set kill switch to OFF.\n\n- [ ] 2. Access battery compartment (under seat or floorboard panel).\n\n- [ ] 3. Inspect battery terminals for white or greenish sulfate corrosion; disconnect NEGATIVE (-) lead first, then POSITIVE (+).\n\n- [ ] 4. Clean cable lugs and lead posts with wire brush and baking soda solution.\n\n- [ ] 5. Reconnect POSITIVE (+) first, then NEGATIVE (-); tighten firmly and torque terminal bolts to 4–5 Nm.\n\n- [ ] 6. Charge battery with a smart trickle charger at 0.8A–1.2A until fully topped at 12.8V DC.\n\n- [ ] 7. Re-test starter push with headlight ON; verify engine cranks briskly.\n\n- [ ] 8. Switch ignition OFF and locate fuse box near battery.\n\n- [ ] 9. Pull the 15A/20A MAIN fuse using plastic fuse puller.\n\n- [ ] 10. Hold fuse against light; inspect internal bridge wire.\n\n- [ ] 11. If broken, inspect wiring harness for pinched or rubbed bare wires grounding against frame.\n\n- [ ] 12. Insert new fuse of the EXACT same ampere rating (never bypass with foil or wire).\n\n- [ ] 13. Turn ignition ON; verify gauge cluster sweeps and headlight illuminates.\n\n**How to verify:**\n\n- Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.\n\n- Stop and seek professional help: If battery will not hold 12.5V after a full 4-hour charge or drops instantly, the internal lead-acid plates have sulfated and battery must be replaced.\n\n- Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.\n\n- Stop and seek professional help: If replacement fuse blows instantly when ignition turns on, a dead short circuit exists; stop and consult a qualified technician.\n\n**Sources in the local library:**\n\n- Battery Council International (BCI) Technical Guidelines & Motorcycle Service Manual Section 16 (Electrical) — reviewed\n\n- OEM Electrical Troubleshooting Guide & SAE J1284 Blade Type Fuses standard — reviewed\n\n_Technical procedure text is kept close to its source wording to avoid changing measurements or safety meaning._\n\n**Limitations:**\n\n- This is offline guidance from stored records, not a confirmed diagnosis.\n\n- The stored record may not cover your exact model, year, or variant. Verify part numbers and specifications against the manufacturer information for your vehicle.\n\n**Next action:** What is your motorcycle make, model, and year? (e.g. Click 125i, NMAX 155, Barako 175).\n\n[chips: Honda Click 125i | Yamaha NMAX 155 | Not sure]','english','{\"reply_text\": \"**Symptom:** My motorcycle engine cranks normally but won\'t start or fire up.\\n\\n> [!NOTE]\\n> Offline knowledge \\u2014 this answer comes from the local reviewed repair library. Gemini was not used.\\n\\n**What I still need to know:**\\n\\n- What is your motorcycle make, model, and year? (e.g. Click 125i, NMAX 155, Barako 175).\\n\\n  - Honda Click 125i\\n\\n  - Yamaha NMAX 155\\n\\n  - Not sure\\n\\n**What this could indicate:**\\n\\n- Discharged or dead 12V motorcycle battery \\u2014 Confidence: high\\n\\n  - Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\\n\\n  - If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\\n\\n- Blown main fuse or disconnected engine ground \\u2014 Confidence: medium\\n\\n  - Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\\n\\n  - Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\\n\\n**Safe checks, in order:**\\n\\n- Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.\\n\\n  - Expected: Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.\\n\\n  - If abnormal: If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.\\n\\n  - Safety first: Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.\\n\\n- Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.\\n\\n  - Expected: Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.\\n\\n  - If abnormal: Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.\\n\\n  - Safety first: Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.\\n\\n**Tools and parts:**\\n\\n- Digital Multimeter (DC Voltage range)\\n\\n- 8mm/10mm box wrench or JIS screwdriver\\n\\n- wire terminal brush.\\n\\n- Fuse puller or needle-nose pliers\\n\\n- replacement 15A/20A mini blade fuse\\n\\n- digital multimeter.\\n\\n**Parts:**\\n\\n- 12V Motorcycle Maintenance-Free Battery (YTX5L-BS / YTZ6V) \\u2014 compatibility NOT verified\\n\\n- Mini Blade Automotive Fuse 15A / 20A \\u2014 compatibility NOT verified\\n\\n**Repair guidance from the record:**\\n\\n_Complete the checks before replacing parts. The steps below are from the stored record and may not match your exact variant._\\n\\n- [ ] 1. Switch motorcycle ignition OFF and set kill switch to OFF.\\n\\n- [ ] 2. Access battery compartment (under seat or floorboard panel).\\n\\n- [ ] 3. Inspect battery terminals for white or greenish sulfate corrosion; disconnect NEGATIVE (-) lead first, then POSITIVE (+).\\n\\n- [ ] 4. Clean cable lugs and lead posts with wire brush and baking soda solution.\\n\\n- [ ] 5. Reconnect POSITIVE (+) first, then NEGATIVE (-); tighten firmly and torque terminal bolts to 4\\u20135 Nm.\\n\\n- [ ] 6. Charge battery with a smart trickle charger at 0.8A\\u20131.2A until fully topped at 12.8V DC.\\n\\n- [ ] 7. Re-test starter push with headlight ON; verify engine cranks briskly.\\n\\n- [ ] 8. Switch ignition OFF and locate fuse box near battery.\\n\\n- [ ] 9. Pull the 15A/20A MAIN fuse using plastic fuse puller.\\n\\n- [ ] 10. Hold fuse against light; inspect internal bridge wire.\\n\\n- [ ] 11. If broken, inspect wiring harness for pinched or rubbed bare wires grounding against frame.\\n\\n- [ ] 12. Insert new fuse of the EXACT same ampere rating (never bypass with foil or wire).\\n\\n- [ ] 13. Turn ignition ON; verify gauge cluster sweeps and headlight illuminates.\\n\\n**How to verify:**\\n\\n- Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.\\n\\n- Stop and seek professional help: If battery will not hold 12.5V after a full 4-hour charge or drops instantly, the internal lead-acid plates have sulfated and battery must be replaced.\\n\\n- Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.\\n\\n- Stop and seek professional help: If replacement fuse blows instantly when ignition turns on, a dead short circuit exists; stop and consult a qualified technician.\\n\\n**Sources in the local library:**\\n\\n- Battery Council International (BCI) Technical Guidelines & Motorcycle Service Manual Section 16 (Electrical) \\u2014 reviewed\\n\\n- OEM Electrical Troubleshooting Guide & SAE J1284 Blade Type Fuses standard \\u