INSERT INTO `troubleshooting_guides` VALUES
(1,1,'Discharged or dead 12V motorcycle battery','High','Measure DC resting voltage across battery terminals using a digital multimeter.','Healthy resting voltage is 12.6V to 12.8V DC. Voltage under starter cranking load must not drop below 10.0V DC.','If voltage reads below 12.0V DC resting or plunges below 9.5V during cranking, battery cells are depleted or damaged.','Digital Multimeter (DC Voltage range), 8mm/10mm box wrench or JIS screwdriver, wire terminal brush.','- [ ] 1. Switch motorcycle ignition OFF and set kill switch to OFF.\n- [ ] 2. Access battery compartment (under seat or floorboard panel).\n- [ ] 3. Inspect battery terminals for white or greenish sulfate corrosion; disconnect NEGATIVE (-) lead first, then POSITIVE (+).\n- [ ] 4. Clean cable lugs and lead posts with wire brush and baking soda solution.\n- [ ] 5. Reconnect POSITIVE (+) first, then NEGATIVE (-); tighten firmly and torque terminal bolts to 4–5 Nm.\n- [ ] 6. Charge battery with a smart trickle charger at 0.8A–1.2A until fully topped at 12.8V DC.\n- [ ] 7. Re-test starter push with headlight ON; verify engine cranks briskly.','Never jump-start a 125cc–150cc scooter with a running car engine; car alternators can overpower motorcycle voltage regulators.','If battery will not hold 12.5V after a full 4-hour charge or drops instantly, the internal lead-acid plates have sulfated and battery must be replaced.','Battery Council International (BCI) Technical Guidelines & Motorcycle Service Manual Section 16 (Electrical)','Battery Council International (BCI) Technical Guidelines & Motorcycle Service Manual Section 16 (Electrical)',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Electrical & Starting','','motorcycle','','','',NULL,NULL,'Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.','',1,'Beginner','2026-11-07 05:07:14.342396','2026-11-07 05:07:14.342731'),
(2,1,'Blown main fuse or disconnected engine ground','Medium','Locate starter relay main fuse holder (typically 15A or 20A green/yellow blade fuse) and test continuity with multimeter.','Continuous beep on multimeter continuity mode (0.0 to 0.1 ohms). Metal link inside transparent fuse must be intact.','Broken metal bridge or black smoke burn inside fuse casing indicates blown circuit due to short or backwards jump.','Fuse puller or needle-nose pliers, replacement 15A/20A mini blade fuse, digital multimeter.','- [ ] 1. Switch ignition OFF and locate fuse box near battery.\n- [ ] 2. Pull the 15A/20A MAIN fuse using plastic fuse puller.\n- [ ] 3. Hold fuse against light; inspect internal bridge wire.\n- [ ] 4. If broken, inspect wiring harness for pinched or rubbed bare wires grounding against frame.\n- [ ] 5. Insert new fuse of the EXACT same ampere rating (never bypass with foil or wire).\n- [ ] 6. Turn ignition ON; verify gauge cluster sweeps and headlight illuminates.','Never substitute a larger fuse (e.g. 30A for 15A); doing so can melt wiring harness and cause vehicle fire.','If replacement fuse blows instantly when ignition turns on, a dead short circuit exists; stop and consult a qualified technician.','OEM Electrical Troubleshooting Guide & SAE J1284 Blade Type Fuses standard','OEM Electrical Troubleshooting Guide & SAE J1284 Blade Type Fuses standard',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Electrical & Starting','','motorcycle','','','',NULL,NULL,'Ensure motorcycle is in Neutral on its center stand before testing electrical components.','',1,'Beginner','2026-11-07 05:07:14.358053','2026-11-07 05:07:14.358319'),
(3,2,'Air trapped inside hydraulic brake lines or degraded brake fluid','High','Squeeze/press brake lever 5 times rapidly. If lever firms up temporarily but goes soft after resting 10 seconds, air is trapped.','Brake lever should become firm within 15–20mm of travel and hold solid pressure for 60 seconds without sinking.','If lever continues to sink slowly while held under steady pressure, internal master cylinder piston cup seals are bypassing.','8mm or 10mm flare nut/box wrench, 1/4-inch clear vinyl tubing, catch bottle, fresh DOT 4 brake fluid (unopened), lint-free shop towels.','- [ ] 1. Park vehicle on flat level ground; turn handlebars to make master cylinder reservoir level.\n- [ ] 2. Wipe reservoir cap clean of dust; remove screws, plastic plate, and rubber diaphragm.\n- [ ] 3. Fill reservoir to UPPER line with fresh DOT 4 fluid from an unopened container.\n- [ ] 4. Slip 8mm box wrench over caliper bleeder nipple, then firmly attach clear vinyl hose leading into catch bottle.\n- [ ] 5. Pump brake lever 3 to 4 times smoothly, then HOLD lever squeezed firmly against grip.\n- [ ] 6. Crack bleeder nipple open 1/4 turn for 1 second; watch fluid and air bubbles expel into hose.\n- [ ] 7. Tighten bleeder nipple securely BEFORE releasing brake lever grip.\n- [ ] 8. Repeat cycle: pump, hold, crack open, close nipple, release lever. Never let reservoir run empty.\n- [ ] 9. Continue until only solid, bubble-free amber fluid emerges.\n- [ ] 10. Top up to UPPER line, reinstall rubber diaphragm and cover. Torque bleeder nipple to 6–8 Nm.','Brake fluid damages painted bodywork instantly. Wash any accidental spill immediately with cold water. Never reuse expelled brake fluid.','If bleeding fails to firm up lever after 10 cycles, master cylinder piston seals or caliper internal seals are damaged and require rebuilding by a certified workshop.','FMVSS 116 / SAE J1704 Brake Fluids & Factory Workshop Hydraulic Service Manual','FMVSS 116 / SAE J1704 Brake Fluids & Factory Workshop Hydraulic Service Manual',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Brake System','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'CRITICAL SAFETY HAZARD: DO NOT RIDE OR DRIVE. Operating a vehicle with soft or spongy brakes can cause catastrophic collision.','',1,'Intermediate','2026-11-07 05:07:14.382872','2026-11-07 05:07:14.383292'),
(4,2,'Worn brake pad friction material past service limit (<2.0mm)','High','Inspect brake caliper window using flashlight to measure remaining pad friction material thickness against steel backing plate.','Pad friction material thickness must be 3.0mm to 5.0mm. Wear indicator groove should be clearly visible.','Friction material under 1.5mm–2.0mm or wear groove gone requires immediate pad replacement before steel plate scores rotor.','Hex/Allen key (5mm/6mm), 12mm/14mm caliper mount socket, brake cleaner spray, C-clamp or piston spreader, 240-grit sandpaper.','- [ ] 1. Support vehicle safely; loosen caliper mounting bolts with socket wrench.\n- [ ] 2. Slide caliper assembly carefully off the brake rotor disc.\n- [ ] 3. Remove pad retaining pins or clips and extract worn brake pads.\n- [ ] 4. Clean caliper piston and slide pins with dedicated residue-free brake cleaner spray.\n- [ ] 5. Push caliper pistons gently and squarely back into bore to clear space for thicker new pads.\n- [ ] 6. Install fresh sintered or ceramic-metallic brake pads; secure retaining pin and cotter clip.\n- [ ] 7. Remount caliper to fork/knuckle; torque mounting bolts to OEM specification (typically 25–30 Nm for bikes, 70–90 Nm for cars).\n- [ ] 8. PUMP BRAKE LEVER/PEDAL 5 TIMES BEFORE MOVING to reseat pads against rotor.','CRITICAL STEP: After installing new pads, you MUST pump the pedal/lever until firm before riding. Otherwise, first brake application will have zero pressure.','If rotor disc has deep grooving (>0.5mm lip) or is discolored blue from severe heat, rotor must be measured with micrometer and resurfaced or replaced.','ISO 611 Road Vehicles Braking Systems & OEM Workshop Brake Service Chapter','ISO 611 Road Vehicles Braking Systems & OEM Workshop Brake Service Chapter',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Brake System','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'CRITICAL SAFETY HAZARD: DO NOT RIDE OR DRIVE. Operating a vehicle with soft or spongy brakes can cause catastrophic collision.','',1,'Beginner','2026-11-07 05:07:14.397569','2026-11-07 05:07:14.397820'),
(5,3,'Cooling fan failure or radiator thermo-switch malfunction','High','With engine fully warmed at idle, disconnect fan thermo-switch connector and jump terminals with fused wire. Check if fan motor spins.','Fan motor should spin vigorously with full RPM and blow air backward through radiator matrix.','If jumped fan does not spin, check 10A fan fuse in fuse box. If fuse is good, fan motor internal brushes or winding has burned out.','Jumper test wire, digital multimeter, 10mm socket, fresh 50/50 ethylene glycol pre-mixed coolant.','- [ ] 1. SHUT OFF ENGINE IMMEDIATELY and park in shade. Wait 45–60 minutes until radiator is cold to the touch.\n- [ ] 2. Check radiator cooling fins for dirt, mud caking, or bent fins; spray clean gently with water hose from back to front.\n- [ ] 3. Inspect coolant expansion tank level; ensure level is between MIN and MAX marks.\n- [ ] 4. Once cold, slowly turn radiator cap counter-clockwise to first stop to release residual vapor, then press down and remove.\n- [ ] 5. Top up radiator neck with 50/50 pre-diluted motorcycle/automotive aluminum-safe coolant.\n- [ ] 6. Inspect fan wiring plug; verify tight electrical connection and absence of melted pins.\n- [ ] 7. Start engine with radiator cap off; observe coolant circulation as engine warms to bleed trapped air pockets.\n- [ ] 8. Install radiator cap tightly past the safety detent.','Never add tap water or mineral drinking water to modern aluminum radiators; minerals cause scale clogging and electrolytic corrosion.','If white sweet-smelling smoke billows continuously from exhaust or oil on dipstick looks like milky coffee, cylinder head gasket has blown; tow to workshop.','SAE J814 Engine Coolants / ASTM D3306 & Manufacturer Liquid Cooling Service Chapter','SAE J814 Engine Coolants / ASTM D3306 & Manufacturer Liquid Cooling Service Chapter',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Cooling System','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'SEVERE SCALD HAZARD: NEVER OPEN A HOT RADIATOR CAP. Boiling pressurized coolant will violently erupt causing severe burns. Allow engine to cool completely for at least 45 minutes.','',1,'Intermediate','2026-11-07 05:07:14.431912','2026-11-07 05:07:14.432300'),
(6,3,'Stuck closed coolant thermostat valve','Medium','Feel upper vs lower radiator hoses after engine reaches operating temperature (use heat-resistant glove).','Both upper and lower radiator hoses should feel uniformly hot (approx 80°C–90°C), confirming thermostat opened.','If upper engine hose is scalding hot while lower radiator hose remains cold to ambient, thermostat is stuck closed blocking coolant flow.','10mm/12mm socket wrench, catch drain pan, new thermostat with rubber O-ring seal, torque wrench.','- [ ] 1. Allow engine to cool completely (cold to touch).\n- [ ] 2. Position drain pan below water pump drain bolt and drain coolant into clean container.\n- [ ] 3. Unbolt thermostat housing (usually 2 bolts, 10mm head).\n- [ ] 4. Extract old thermostat; note direction of jiggle pin / air bleed hole (points upward).\n- [ ] 5. Bench test: place thermostat in pot of water with thermometer; valve must open noticeably at 82°C–88°C.\n- [ ] 6. Fit new thermostat with fresh rubber gasket into housing.\n- [ ] 7. Tighten housing bolts to 10–12 Nm; refill system with fresh coolant and burp air pockets.','Always replace the rubber gasket/O-ring when changing thermostat; reusing old crushed gaskets causes persistent leaks.','If engine continues to overheat after new thermostat and fan are confirmed working, water pump impeller has stripped or radiator core is calcified.','OEM Cooling System Service Manual & Gates Automotive Thermostat Diagnostic Guide','OEM Cooling System Service Manual & Gates Automotive Thermostat Diagnostic Guide',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Cooling System','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'SEVERE SCALD HAZARD: NEVER OPEN A HOT RADIATOR CAP. Boiling pressurized coolant will violently erupt causing severe burns. Allow engine to cool completely for at least 45 minutes.','',1,'Intermediate','2026-11-07 05:07:14.448063','2026-11-07 05:07:14.448296'),
(7,4,'Faulty alternator / motorcycle stator coil or rectifier-regulator','High','Start engine and rev to 3,000–4,000 RPM while measuring DC voltage across battery terminals.','Voltage must rise to between 13.8V and 14.8V DC with engine running at 3000 RPM.','If voltage remains at battery resting level (12.2V–12.5V) or drops lower as RPM rises, the charging system is not outputting power.','Digital Multimeter, socket set, terminal cleaner.','- [ ] 1. Check alternator drive belt tension (on cars) or stator 3-pin yellow AC output plug (on bikes).\n- [ ] 2. For motorcycles: unplug 3-pin stator yellow wires; measure AC voltage between all 3 phase pairs (1-2, 2-3, 1-3) at 4000 RPM (expect 40V–60V AC).\n- [ ] 3. Check resistance of stator phases to ground with engine off (must read open circuit / infinite resistance; any continuity to ground means burned stator).\n- [ ] 4. Inspect rectifier-regulator heatsink and plug for melted plastic or corroded prongs.\n- [ ] 5. Clean frame grounding eyelet lug to bare metal and re-torque.\n- [ ] 6. Replace defective regulator/rectifier or rewind stator coil.\n- [ ] 7. Retest charging voltage across battery at 3,000 RPM (must be 14.0V–14.5V DC).','If charging voltage exceeds 15.2V DC, the regulator has failed open and is boiling the battery acid; discontinue riding immediately.','If stator windings show charred black insulation or copper smell, engine side cover must be removed to replace stator assembly.','SAE J56 Electrical Generating Systems & OEM Factory Electrical Manual','SAE J56 Electrical Generating Systems & OEM Factory Electrical Manual',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Charging System','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'Remove metal jewelry and wristwatches before testing alternator/stator terminals to avoid accidental dead-short burns.','',1,'Intermediate','2026-11-07 05:07:14.465334','2026-11-07 05:07:14.465562'),
(8,5,'Clogged carburetor jets (lean mixture) or dirty fuel injector nozzle','High','For carb: test with choke pulled; running better confirms fuel starvation (lean mixture). For EFI: measure fuel rail pressure with pressure gauge (expect 294 kPa / 43 PSI for Honda PGM-FI).','Fuel spray must atomize into a micro-fine conical mist. Carburetor pilot and main brass jet orifices must show clear round pinhole of light.','Irregular dripping spray pattern or obstructed jet pinhole confirms varnish/dirt blockage from stale ethanol blended fuel.','Carburetor cleaner aerosol spray, JIS screwdrivers (No. 1 & 2), 0.3mm soft copper jet cleaning wire, clean glass bowl.','- [ ] 1. Shut fuel valve petcock OFF; place drain bottle under carb float bowl drain tube.\n- [ ] 2. Loosen drain screw; inspect drained fuel for water globules, sediment, or rust particles.\n- [ ] 3. Loosen intake manifold clamp screws and remove carburetor body.\n- [ ] 4. Remove float bowl; carefully unscrew pilot (slow) jet and main jet using exact-fit flat screwdriver.\n- [ ] 5. Spray carburetor cleaner through jet orifices; verify light passes cleanly through center hole and side emulsion holes.\n- [ ] 6. Spray clean fuel inlet passageway and float needle valve seat.\n- [ ] 7. Inspect rubber intake manifold boot for hairline dry-rot cracks that allow unmetered vacuum leak.\n- [ ] 8. Reassemble float bowl with clean O-ring; reinstall carb and adjust fuel mixture pilot screw to factory baseline (typically 1.5 to 2.25 turns out).','Never use hard steel wire or drill bits to poke carburetor brass jets; scratching the precision orifice permanently alters fuel calibration.','On EFI vehicles, if fuel pump does not prime with a 2-second hum when key turns on, test fuel pump relay and pump winding resistance before disassembling throttle body.','Mikuni/Keihin Carburetor Tuning Manual & Bosch Automotive Gasoline Injection Handbook','Mikuni/Keihin Carburetor Tuning Manual & Bosch Automotive Gasoline Injection Handbook',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Fuel & Air Intake','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'Work in well-ventilated open air away from open flames, pilot lights, and smoking materials.','',1,'Intermediate','2026-11-07 05:07:14.486552','2026-11-07 05:07:14.486786'),
(9,5,'Worn CVT drive belt or slipping automatic clutch pads (Scooters / Automatics)','Medium','Hold rear brake firmly on flat surface and apply smooth throttle for 2 seconds. If engine RPM races up high but scooter barely pulls forward, clutch/CVT is slipping.','CVT drive belt width must be within specification (e.g. Honda Click 125/150: minimum 18.5mm service limit, new 19.5mm).','Belt width below service limit, worn pulley ramps, or clutch bell glazed with blue burn marks causes severe acceleration slippage.','8mm T-handle, 17mm & 19mm socket, CVT variator locking holder tool or impact wrench, vernier caliper.','- [ ] 1. Remove 8mm bolts securing CVT crankcase cover.\n- [ ] 2. Remove drive face and clutch bell using variator holding tool.\n- [ ] 3. Measure drive belt width with vernier caliper; inspect belt teeth for fraying, rubber cracks, or missing cogs.\n- [ ] 4. Remove variator movable face; inspect roller weights for flat spots (replace if flat-spotted).\n- [ ] 5. Inspect clutch bell interior for oil contamination or glaze; scuff lightly with 180-grit sandpaper.\n- [ ] 6. Clean all belt dust with brake cleaner spray and lint-free towel.\n- [ ] 7. Reassemble variator, new belt, and clutch; torque drive pulley nut to OEM spec (59 Nm) and clutch nut (49 Nm).','Never get grease or oil onto CVT pulleys or belt; grease causes instant uncontrollable drive slippage.','If clutch bell has visible cracks or is warped out of round (>0.2mm), it must be replaced to prevent catastrophic disintegration at high RPM.','Honda V-Matic Transmission System Service Manual / Yamaha CVT Technician Guide','Honda V-Matic Transmission System Service Manual / Yamaha CVT Technician Guide',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Fuel & Air Intake','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'Work in well-ventilated open air away from open flames, pilot lights, and smoking materials.','',1,'Intermediate','2026-11-07 05:07:14.502947','2026-11-07 05:07:14.503182'),
(10,6,'Excessive valve tappet clearance (Loose intake/exhaust valve rocker)','High','Measure gap between valve stem tip and rocker arm screw with precision steel feeler gauge at Top Dead Center (TDC) compression stroke.','Typical clearance for modern 125–150cc engines: Intake 0.08mm–0.12mm; Exhaust 0.12mm–0.16mm (check exact engine spec).','Clearance exceeding 0.20mm causes distinct sewing-machine / typewriter rhythmic ticking that doubles in frequency as RPM rises.','Metric feeler gauge set (0.04mm–0.30mm), 8mm/9mm tappet locknut box wrench, valve adjustment square/slotted tool, 14mm/17mm crankshaft turning socket.','- [ ] 1. Ensure engine is COMPLETELY COLD (below 35°C); measuring hot valves gives dangerously incorrect clearance.\n- [ ] 2. Remove valve tappet inspection covers or cylinder head cover.\n- [ ] 3. Remove timing inspection plug on alternator cover; rotate crankshaft counter-clockwise until \'T\' mark aligns with index notch on compression stroke.\n- [ ] 4. Confirm both intake and exhaust rocker arms have slight free wiggle (both valves fully closed).\n- [ ] 5. Insert feeler gauge blade between valve stem tip and adjustment screw; feeler should slide with slight drag (like pulling paper from a book).\n- [ ] 6. If loose, loosen locknut, turn adjustment screw until correct drag is achieved, then tighten locknut while holding screw steady.\n- [ ] 7. Re-check clearance after tightening locknut; rotate crankshaft 720 degrees and re-verify at TDC.\n- [ ] 8. Reinstall inspection caps with clean O-rings (torque 12 Nm).','A slightly noisy valve is safer than an overly tight valve. Too-tight valves will burn and destroy cylinder head compression.','If valve adjuster screw is bottomed out and clearance is still excessive, valve stem tip or rocker arm pad is severely worn and requires head disassembly.','OEM Factory Engine Mechanical Service Chapter & SAE Engine Valvetrain Diagnostic Guide','OEM Factory Engine Mechanical Service Chapter & SAE Engine Valvetrain Diagnostic Guide',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Engine Mechanical','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'IF NOISE IS A DEEP HEAVY KNOCK FROM CRANKCASE, SHUT OFF ENGINE IMMEDIATELY. Continued running will throw connecting rod through crankcase, destroying the engine and locking wheels.','',1,'Intermediate','2026-11-07 05:07:14.526110','2026-11-07 05:07:14.526341'),
(11,6,'Worn or collapsed Cam Chain Tensioner (CCT) / Loose timing chain','High','Listen to side cylinder casting with a mechanic stethoscope or long screwdriver pressed against ear; observe if clatter quiets under light RPM load.','Automatic cam chain tensioner plunger should ratchet outward and hold firm spring pressure against cam chain guide.','If tensioner internal spring has snapped, plunger retracts under chain slap, causing rapid clattering noise against cylinder tunnel.','8mm socket wrench, small flat screwdriver, fresh tensioner gasket, replacement OEM automatic tensioner.','- [ ] 1. Clean dirt around tensioner body on rear/side of cylinder block.\n- [ ] 2. Remove center cap bolt on tensioner; insert small flat screwdriver into internal tensioner screw.\n- [ ] 3. Turn clockwise to retract plunger; if tensioner spring has zero counter-force, spring is broken.\n- [ ] 4. Remove two 8mm mounting bolts and extract tensioner body.\n- [ ] 5. Prepare new tensioner: retract plunger fully clockwise until locked.\n- [ ] 6. Install new tensioner with fresh gasket onto cylinder block; tighten mounting bolts to 10 Nm.\n- [ ] 7. Release internal screw (turn counter-clockwise) so plunger firmly snaps against timing chain guide.\n- [ ] 8. Reinstall center sealing cap and start engine; verify clatter has completely disappeared.','Never operate engine with a loose cam chain; loose chain can skip timing teeth, causing piston to smash into open valves, destroying the engine.','If chain continues to rattle after installing new tensioner, the timing chain itself has stretched beyond wear limits and must be replaced along with sprockets.','OEM Valvetrain Timing & Cam Chain Service Chapter','OEM Valvetrain Timing & Cam Chain Service Chapter',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Engine Mechanical','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'IF NOISE IS A DEEP HEAVY KNOCK FROM CRANKCASE, SHUT OFF ENGINE IMMEDIATELY. Continued running will throw connecting rod through crankcase, destroying the engine and locking wheels.','',1,'Intermediate','2026-11-07 05:07:14.539637','2026-11-07 05:07:14.539886'),
(12,6,'Connecting rod big-end bearing failure (Rod Knock / Crankshaft bearing damage)','High','SHUT OFF ENGINE IMMEDIATELY. Inspect oil dipstick or drain plug magnet for brass/bronze/aluminum glitter flakes.','Clean engine oil without glitter, smooth quiet bottom-end rotation with zero play on connecting rod journal.','Bronze or silver metal flakes in oil combined with dull heavy mechanical knock indicates ruined rod bearing journals.','Drain pan, 12mm/17mm socket, flashlight, magnet wand.','- [ ] 1. DO NOT START OR RIDE THE VEHICLE. Continued operation will seize the crankshaft and fracture engine crankcase.\n- [ ] 2. Drain 100ml of engine oil into a clean white paper cup.\n- [ ] 3. Shine a flashlight onto oil surface; examine for metallic glitter, bronze particles, or aluminum sludge.\n- [ ] 4. Run a clean neodymium magnet through drained oil to check for ferrous steel shavings.\n- [ ] 5. If glitter is present, rod bearing shells or crankpin needle bearings have disintegrated.\n- [ ] 6. Secure vehicle for transport to a qualified engine rebuilding workshop for full crankshaft overhaul.','There is NO chemical additive, thicker oil, or external adjustment that can fix rod bearing knock. Full engine teardown is required.','STOP IMMEDIATELY. Attempting to ride will result in thrown rod and catastrophic total loss.','SAE Technical Paper Series: Crankshaft Journal Bearing Wear & OEM Heavy Engine Overhaul Manual','SAE Technical Paper Series: Crankshaft Journal Bearing Wear & OEM Heavy Engine Overhaul Manual',NULL,'approved',NULL,NULL,'Imported from the bundled knowledge file. Re-check the source before using it on a different model or variant.','Engine Mechanical','','all','','','',NULL,NULL,'IF NOISE IS A DEEP HEAVY KNOCK FROM CRANKCASE, SHUT OFF ENGINE IMMEDIATELY. Continued running will throw connecting rod through crankcase, destroying the engine and locking wheels.','',1,'Advanced','2026-11-07 05:07:14.556725','2026-11-07 05:07:14.557131');
